```java
public class PowerOfThree {

    public static boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n < 0 || (n!= 0 && n % 9!= 0)) {
            return false;
        }
        return Math.pow(Math.abs(n), (1.0 / 3)) == Math.floor(Math.abs(n));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isPowerOfThree(27)); //true
        System.out.println(isPowerOfThree(0)); //false
        System.out.println(isPowerOfThree(-1));//false
    }
}
```

This solution works by first checking if `n` is negative or not divisible by nine. If either condition fails, then `n` cannot be a power of three and we can immediately return `false`.

If both conditions pass, we take the absolute value of `n`, find its cube-root using `Math.pow()`, and compare it with the expected cube-root. We do this because some numbers have multiple cube-similar but different roots (`6` has two cube-similar roots, one of them being `-2`). Hence, to avoid confusion around this edge case, we find the floor of `n`. Finally, we check whether their difference is more than zero or not, i.e., they're distinct. If so, then it means `n` isn't a power of three and we return `false`.

